WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.434
In our 2024 lead,

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.936
Republican presidential hopeful

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:03.503
Governor Nikki

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:04.537
Haley made a surprise

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.606
appearance on NBC's Saturday

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:07.674
Night Live this weekend,

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.375
where she hit

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:12.412
Trump on his mental fitness and his age.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:13.580
Are you doing okay?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.014
Donald,

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:16.282
you might need a mental competency test.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:17.083
You know what I did?

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:17.884
I took the test

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.452
and I said, okay, perfect score.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:23.123
They said, I'm 100% mental.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:24.724
As he tries to narrow the gap

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:25.492
between her

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:26.659
and Trump in South Carolina's

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.395
Republican presidential primary, CNN's

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:29.229
Kylie Atwood

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.130
takes a look now

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.400
at how Haley's grumpy old men attacks

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.469
against Trump and Biden are playing among

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:40.240
South Carolina's retirees.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.242
These are people making decisions

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.244
on our national security.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:45.812
These are people making decisions

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.113
on the future of our economy.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:47.881
We need to know

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.582
they're at the top of their game.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:50.283
Nikki Haley

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.318
not backing away from her argument

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:53.887
that the American president

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.422
shouldn't be in their eighties.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:58.258
Mandatory mental competency tests

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:02.028
for politicians over 75 years old.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.596
It has been a critical piece

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:04.764
of the 52 year old's

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.866
pitch to voters from day one.

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.802
One that she has both sharpened.

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:10.236
Why are we allowing ourselves

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.671
to have 280 year olds

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:12.572
who can't serve

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.440
eight years,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.874
who both are diminished,

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.776
whether it's in their character

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.778
or in their mental capacity

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.980
and played with in recent weeks.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:21.548
Sixth Sense

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:25.418
Remember that when I see dead people?

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:25.652
Yeah,

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:26.986
that's what voters will say

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.888
if they see you and Joe on the ballot.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.490
How often to an audience

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.825
filled with retirees

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:33.026
like this bar in Hilton

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:34.494
Head, South Carolina?

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.697
I just don't think our country should be

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:40.934
with somebody who's going

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.369
on its way out

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.873
when we still have so much young blood

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.242
for 69 year old Maureen Bolger.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.544
The idea of moving to a new generation

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.012
is energizing.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.947
South Carolina

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.950
was the fastest growing state in 2023,

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.651
largely because of an influx

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.087
of almost 40,000 retirees.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.422
And Haley is betting that

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:03.756
they get her argument.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.592
I think older people see it, too.

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:07.460
61 year old Anna memo

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.529
also fits that target audience.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:10.964
Whether it's the Biden ticket

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:11.865
or the Trump ticket,

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:14.334
I do feel that it's very important

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.301
to look at age

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.769
and consider age and

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:20.173
and cognitive cognitive skills.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:20.773
But not

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:22.575
everyone considering the state's former

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.711
governor found it to be the best.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:27.046
I do think that we still have people

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.047
that are

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.282
78 and 80

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.684
that can be senators and representatives

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:32.986
for Edward Spears,

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.555
currently an undecided GOP voter.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.090
It's just a part of the game.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.658
Used to be elected.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.593
If I was a younger candidate,

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:41.561
I would do the same thing.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.062
It's just a strategy.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.797
And for older Trump supporters,

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.432
even those interested in Haley,

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:47.467
like Carol and Greg

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:48.301
Carney,

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.936
who moved full time to Hilton

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.237
Head nine years ago.

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.605
I think she's a need person.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:53.640
We read her book.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.109
The tactic of going after Trump's age

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.178
hasn't been a decisive factor

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.879
because they are squarely set

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.915
on voting for the former president,

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.850
say casting the seniors.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.852
And that's not right

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:06.920
because we're individuals.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.788
But if she weren't doing these age

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:09.155
things, it's

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.490
not like you would go for her

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.424
if she had lost

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.226
that argument in the past.

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:14.794
But if Trump were not running, yes,

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:20.833
I would run. I'm old and I'm stubborn.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.334
Now, Jake,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:21.768
as you can

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:23.069
see from our conversations

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.470
with older voters, most of them

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.705
who are looking for

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.839
an alternative to Trump,

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:27.941
pretty much well received

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:28.908
those age arguments

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:29.943
that Haley has been making.

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:30.743
The question is

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:31.711
how many of them

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.046
are actually here in South Carolina

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:35.415
where Trump remains quite popular?

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:36.983
And the other thing I want to note on

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:37.951
fundraising, Haley's

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.485
campaign said over the weekend

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:41.221
that in the month of January,

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:45.792
they hold in $16.5 million in donations.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.760
That is their biggest fundraising month

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.361
to date,

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.096
giving them a significant financial

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.464
both heading into South Carolina

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:52.999
primary in less than three weeks

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.468
and potentially beyond Jake.

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:55.835
All right.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.203
Kylie Atwood

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:58.738
in Spartanburg, South Carolina,

00:03:58.738 --> 00:03:59.272
thanks so much.

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:00.406
Let's bring in our political panel

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.140
to weigh in on this

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.242
and so much more

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:02.942
for former

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.911
Democratic Congressman Max Rose.

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:06.412
Haley's called for a mental competency

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.648
test for politicians over 75,

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:11.484
but in her SNL appearance this weekend,

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.052
she turned her attack line on Trump

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.188
and Biden's age into into a punch line.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:18.725
Do you think that I mean,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:19.125
do you think

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.193
it's ageism and

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.161
do you think it will hurt her?

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.763
It it doesn't really matter.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.231
She her campaign is over.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.566
I mean, statistically speaking,

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:29.068
she's going to lose South Carolina

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.104
by 20, 30 points.

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:32.038
And right now,

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:33.139
she's being funded

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:36.142
by an array of anti-Trump forces.

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:36.909
Obviously,

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.779
of which I consider myself one.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:41.347
What's fascinating, though,

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:43.683
about the overarching dynamic

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:45.985
is this is actually quite helpful

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:47.353
for Donald Trump.

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:47.687
You know,

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:48.688
people are forgetting

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:50.823
the disastrous four years

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.525
that was the Trump presidency.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.127
And that's why you're seeing an uptick

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.795
in his general election numbers.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.964
The sooner that this primary is over,

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.465
the sooner that people can understand

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.534
this election is a very simple

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.804
binary choice between crazy and normalcy.

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:07.707
And the Biden campaign is looking forward

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:08.441
to that contest.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:08.741
And see,

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:09.509
according to its latest

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:10.910
filing with the FEC,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:11.744
the Federal Election Commission,

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.946
the RNC has only $8 million

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:14.914
in cash on hand.

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.015
That's the party's lowest

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.583
fundraising in a decade.

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:18.685
The DNC,

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:19.619
the Democratic National Committee's

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.321
$21 million cash on hand

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:23.389
yesterday in an interview on Fox.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:26.592
Former President Trump suggested that the

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.928
he alluded to possibly the RNC chair,

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:29.962
Ronna McDaniel,

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:32.265
who's been very loyal to him.

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:32.899
He alluded to

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:35.902
her leaving perhaps unwillingly.

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.037
Take a listen.

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:40.273
How's Ronna McDaniel doing?

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:41.207
I think she did great

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:43.009
when she ran Michigan for me.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:44.377
I think she did okay

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.045
initially in the RNC.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:48.047
I would say right now

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:51.951
they'll probably be some changes made.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:52.952
That's a polite

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.821
it's a polite version of Donald Trump.

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.122
But he's basically suggesting

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:57.123
that she's on her way out

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:57.924
and that he's going to be

00:05:57.924 --> 00:05:59.559
a big part of why.

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.861
Yeah, And would anyone be surprised

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:02.995
if Trump just decided

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.630
I'll be the interim de facto

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:05.732
head of the RNC?

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:07.100
I mean, I wouldn't.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.735
And it almost doesn't matter who

00:06:08.735 --> 00:06:09.702
he installs there,

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.404
because it will be someone

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:13.206
that will do everything he says.

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.974
But I'm just reminded

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.744
of that September in 2015,

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:19.112
when Reince Priebus, the

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:22.115
then head of the RNC, blew up to New York

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:23.583
to beg Donald Trump

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:25.385
to sign a loyalty pledge.

00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:25.985
What should have been

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:26.953
the other way around?

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:27.453
You know,

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:28.187
Donald Trump

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:30.356
should have been begging the RNC,

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:31.624
give me your support

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:32.859
and all of your infrastructure

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:33.593
and resources,

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:34.527
even though I'm a very

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.062
unconventional candidate.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.964
And I think the flip of that

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:40.133
really just foretold

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.434
what was going to happen

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:42.602
to the Republican Party

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.504
and the RNC, a total Trump takeover.

00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:45.438
We have with us today

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:46.672
for the first time, Coleman Hughes.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:47.373
Congratulations.

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.842
You have a brand new book out tomorrow.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.011
It is called The End of Race

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.212
Politics Arguments

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:54.614
for a Colorblind America.

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:55.114
In the book,

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:55.415
you write,

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.650
among other things, quote, Colorblindness

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.252
is the wisest principle

00:06:59.252 --> 00:06:59.585
by which

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:01.387
to govern our fragile experiment

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.589
in multi-ethnic democracy.

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:04.891
Can you explain the case

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:06.392
you're making for a colorblind society?

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:07.727
And the obvious rebuttal,

00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:10.062
which is that sounds nice,

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:13.666
but you have institutionalized racism

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:15.168
in housing

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:16.969
and in other parts of the country

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:18.604
that make that impossible.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:18.805
Yes.

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:22.275
So colorblindness used to be the standard

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.342
liberal philosophy

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:24.644
for a moment in the sixties

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:26.379
when we passed the Civil Rights Act.

00:07:26.379 --> 00:07:27.680
The idea was

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.182
that we are going to get race

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:30.750
out of public policy

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:31.818
and we're going to deal

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:33.386
with the legacy of slavery,

00:07:33.386 --> 00:07:36.489
with racial inequality, by an intense

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.690
anti-poverty program

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.158
that would benefit the black poor

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:40.626
and the white poor alike,

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:42.595
but would disproportionately benefit

00:07:42.595 --> 00:07:44.163
the black and Hispanic poor

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:45.531
because blacks and Hispanics

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:47.533
are disproportionately likely to be poor.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:47.800
Right.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:48.734
But then we could do all that

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.937
on the basis of socioeconomic class

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:52.104
rather than race.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:52.605
In the past

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:53.439
50 years,

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:54.974
there have been a group of scholars,

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:56.776
and especially in the past ten years,

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:58.377
it's become much more popular

00:07:58.377 --> 00:07:59.879
with the rise of critical race theory

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:00.913
and so forth.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.582
To say that actually colorblindness

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:04.183
is not the right way to go.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:06.819
It's a Trojan horse for white supremacy.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.221
All it does is allow

00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:10.056
racial inequality to persist.

00:08:10.056 --> 00:08:11.324
So what we have to do

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:12.291
is aggressively

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:14.727
put race into every public policy,

00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:16.329
including even COVID

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:17.430
emergency policies

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.398
that prioritized emergency

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:20.933
aid based on race.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:21.901
I argue that

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.269
actually the civil rights movement

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:24.170
had it right.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.538
If you go back, you read Martin

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.440
Luther King's book, Why We Can't Wait.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.474
He says, yes,

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:30.543
we have to address the legacy of slavery.

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:32.979
Yes, we have to do something special

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.514
in order to repay the debt

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:35.381
to black Americans.

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:37.149
But that something should be

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:39.619
socioeconomic class based policy

00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:40.820
doesn't discriminate

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:42.822
against anyone on the basis of skin color

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.457
and still tackles poverty.

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.325
That that should I think liberals

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:47.727
and Democrats should reclaim

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:49.529
that as a default position.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:52.431
So race and the civil War

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:54.000
specifically have been a topic

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.434
that have come up in the 2024

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:56.702
presidential election.

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:57.403
Here's another clip

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.204
from Nikki Haley

00:08:58.204 --> 00:08:59.939
trying to make light of

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:03.543
her fumbled answer. A few weeks ago.

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:04.844
I was just curious, what

00:09:04.844 --> 00:09:05.444
would you say

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:08.447
was the main cause of the Civil War?

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:09.815
And do you think it starts with

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:13.586
an S and ends with slavery?

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:15.221
Yeah, I probably should have said that

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:16.956
the first time.

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:18.157
Now, in your book, you argue

00:09:18.157 --> 00:09:19.392
slavery is one of the most heinous

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:20.259
examples of race

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:21.961
based policies in the country.

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:22.562
I'm curious

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:23.996
as to what you make of the fact

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:25.698
that this is still so awkward

00:09:25.698 --> 00:09:28.100
for some people to talk about.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.435
Like Governor Haley,

00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:31.137
when she gave that first answer

00:09:31.137 --> 00:09:32.672
and did not mention slavery

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:33.139
when asked what

00:09:33.139 --> 00:09:34.340
the cause of the Civil War was.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.808
Well, I'm glad she corrected it.

00:09:35.808 --> 00:09:36.576
The common

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:37.877
talking point on the other side

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:38.377
has been that

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:38.945
the Civil War

00:09:38.945 --> 00:09:40.680
is really about states rights.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.148
Now, the easiest way to know

00:09:42.148 --> 00:09:43.082
that that's not true

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:46.953
is because when northern states wanted to

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:47.553
to fight

00:09:47.553 --> 00:09:49.188
fugitive slave laws, in other words,

00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:50.890
they didn't want to have to

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.358
essentially extradite slaves

00:09:52.358 --> 00:09:53.726
that escape from the south

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:55.995
back to the south.

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:56.362
No one

00:09:56.362 --> 00:09:57.663
in the south said, actually,

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:58.731
those northern states

00:09:58.731 --> 00:10:00.132
have their own states rights

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:02.134
and they don't have to extradite

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:03.736
those those.

00:10:03.736 --> 00:10:05.404
It wasn't about some principle concern

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:06.539
about states rights.

00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:07.506
It was about the clash

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:08.608
between half the country

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:10.242
that wanted to be a slave society

00:10:10.242 --> 00:10:11.844
and the other half which didn't.

00:10:11.844 --> 00:10:12.945
All right.

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:14.880
So, so good to have you on the show.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:15.548
We'll have you back.

00:10:15.548 --> 00:10:17.883
And congratulations again on your book.

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:18.751
Really appreciate it.

